meta:
  title: Machine-Readable Specification for Local Model Runtime Service
  version: '1.0'
  source_spec: docs/plans/source_spec.md
  status: draft
  description: Structured projection of the LMRS HRS into concepts, typed 
    relations, and source label coverage.
concepts:
- concept_id: C-001
  name: Local Model Runtime Service
  definition: A standalone adapter-based microservice that provides high-level 
    access to locally executed language models while enforcing prompt admission 
    before runtime execution.
  properties:
  - It is separate from model_access_core and ollama/model_workspace.
  - It exposes local model access through an adapter-based MCP interface.
  - It must prevent any prompt from reaching the local model runtime until 
    context-window and dynamic VRAM capacity have been proven.
  source_labels:
  - '{lmrs}'
  - '{goal}'
  - '{inv1}'
- concept_id: C-002
  name: Provider Gateway Integration
  definition: The integration boundary through which model_access_core routes 
    local provider requests to the Local Model Runtime Service.
  properties:
  - model_access_core remains the universal provider gateway.
  - model_access_core does not manage VRAM, load local models, or execute local 
    prompts directly.
  - LMRS appears to model_access_core as a local provider backend and returns 
    responses normalizable into canonical provider responses.
  source_labels:
  - '{sep1}'
  - '{prov}'
- concept_id: C-003
  name: Adapter-Based MCP Interface
  definition: The public MCP command surface implemented according to 
    mcp_proxy_adapter conventions.
  properties:
  - The service is built on mcp_proxy_adapter principles.
  - Public commands expose schemas, validation, execution, metadata, structured 
    responses, structured errors, healthcheck, and MCP Proxy registration.
  source_labels:
  - '{adpt}'
- concept_id: C-004
  name: Canonical Chat Request
  definition: The request structure accepted from the provider gateway for local
    model execution or admission decisions.
  properties:
  - It contains session_id, request_id, model_name, messages, tools, 
    reserved_output_tokens, and options.
  - It is close to the canonical chat request used by the provider gateway.
  source_labels:
  - '{prov}'
- concept_id: C-005
  name: Model Profile
  definition: A model-specific runtime description that makes LMRS independent 
    from any one local model.
  properties:
  - It includes model_name, runtime backend, path or endpoint, declared context 
    window, tokenizer profile, load policy, KV-cache profile, and concurrency 
    policy.
  - Qwen, Ollama models, GPT-OSS, and other local models are instances described
    by profiles.
  source_labels:
  - '{mdl1}'
- concept_id: C-006
  name: Hardware Profile
  definition: The hardware capacity description used for admission and 
    calibration decisions.
  properties:
  - The initial target profile is one GPU with 24 GB VRAM and 128 GB RAM.
  - The architecture must allow future larger VRAM cards and multiple GPUs 
    without changing the external provider contract.
  source_labels:
  - '{hw01}'
- concept_id: C-007
  name: Resident GPU Services Baseline
  definition: The measured GPU baseline after always-on GPU resident services 
    are running and before the main LLM is loaded.
  properties:
  - Resident services may include vectorizer, chunker, CUDA/runtime buffers, and
    other always-on processes.
  - The baseline is not an empty GPU state.
  - The service measures service_baseline_free_vram at start or calibration.
  source_labels:
  - '{base}'
  - '{mem1}'
- concept_id: C-008
  name: VRAM Runtime Facts
  definition: Measured runtime facts describing baseline free VRAM, model-loaded
    free VRAM, measured model static VRAM, and dynamic pool capacity.
  properties:
  - model_loaded_free_vram is measured after loading the local model.
  - measured_model_static_vram is the difference between 
    service_baseline_free_vram and model_loaded_free_vram.
  - Measured static VRAM is stored for model_name, runtime, 
    quantization/profile, and hardware_profile.
  - Runtime facts must not be replaced by purely theoretical assumptions.
  source_labels:
  - '{load}'
  - '{stat}'
  - '{inv3}'
- concept_id: C-009
  name: Dynamic VRAM Pool
  definition: The remaining VRAM capacity available for KV-cache, runtime 
    buffers, batching overhead, and active request reservations after resident 
    services and model static memory.
  properties:
  - Maximum dynamic pool is computed from free VRAM after model load minus 
    safety margin and runtime reserve.
  - Current dynamic pool additionally subtracts active request reservations.
  - The dynamic pool bounds whether requests execute immediately, queue, or 
    reject.
  source_labels:
  - '{dyn1}'
  - '{dyn2}'
  - '{lim3}'
- concept_id: C-010
  name: Token Accounting
  definition: The tokenizer-aware accounting process that computes request token
    requirements before queuing and before runtime execution.
  properties:
  - Token counting covers final prompt/chat-template content, system messages, 
    role markers, assistant/user messages, tool schemas, service headers, and 
    reserved output tokens.
  - The preferred mode uses the tokenizer matching the executing model and 
    runtime.
  - Rough chars/4 estimates are allowed only as explicitly marked fallback.
  - If exact tokenizer is unavailable, the service either uses explicitly 
    allowed approximate mode or rejects with TOKENIZER_UNAVAILABLE.
  source_labels:
  - '{tok1}'
  - '{tok2}'
  - '{tok3}'
- concept_id: C-011
  name: Request Capacity Estimate
  definition: The computed token and dynamic VRAM requirement for an individual 
    request.
  properties:
  - required_tokens includes input tokens, tool tokens, service tokens, and 
    reserved output tokens.
  - Requests exceeding the declared model context window are rejected.
  - Dynamic VRAM estimate is based on required_tokens, calibrated KV-cache cost 
    per token, and per-request overhead.
  - Allowed request size is the minimum of declared context window and 
    dynamic-pool token capacity.
  source_labels:
  - '{lim1}'
  - '{lim2}'
  - '{lim3}'
- concept_id: C-012
  name: Admission Control
  definition: The pre-queue decision process that classifies requests as 
    executable, queueable, or rejected using context-window and VRAM capacity 
    constraints.
  properties:
  - Admission runs before a request enters the queue.
  - Requests over the model context window are rejected with CONTEXT_OVERFLOW.
  - Requests too large for the maximum dynamic pool are rejected with 
    REQUEST_TOO_LARGE or HARDWARE_VRAM_INSUFFICIENT.
  - Requests that fit the maximum dynamic pool but not current capacity are 
    queued, not permanently rejected.
  - Requests fitting both model window and current dynamic pool may execute 
    immediately or be queued according to scheduler policy.
  - LMCache reuse does not permit unsafe admission.
  source_labels:
  - '{adm1}'
  - '{adm2}'
  - '{adm3}'
  - '{inv1}'
  - '{inv2}'
  - '{lmcache2}'
  - '{lmcache6}'
  - '{inv5}'
- concept_id: C-013
  name: Request Queue
  definition: The queue of admitted requests waiting for dynamic VRAM capacity 
    or scheduler selection.
  properties:
  - Queued records store request_id, session_id, model_name, token counts, 
    required dynamic VRAM, admission timestamp, expiration timestamp, priority, 
    and status.
  - Queued requests must pass capacity recheck before actual launch.
  source_labels:
  - '{que1}'
  - '{rech}'
- concept_id: C-014
  name: Scheduler Policy
  definition: The policy that selects queued requests for execution when dynamic
    VRAM becomes available.
  properties:
  - The primary policy is largest-fit scheduling.
  - Largest-fit selects the largest queued request that fits the current dynamic
    pool.
  - FIFO is allowed only as fallback or a configurable mode.
  source_labels:
  - '{que2}'
  - '{que3}'
- concept_id: C-015
  name: Runtime Backend
  definition: An execution backend abstraction that runs only requests admitted 
    by LMRS.
  properties:
  - Supported backend examples include vLLM, Ollama, llama.cpp, GPT-OSS runtime,
    or another local runtime.
  - The runtime backend does not make admission decisions.
  source_labels:
  - '{run1}'
- concept_id: C-016
  name: Runtime Client
  definition: The abstraction that hides backend differences and returns 
    normalized local model execution results.
  properties:
  - The runtime client returns assistant message, usage, status, runtime 
    metadata, and telemetry.
  - The result must be suitable for normalization back into a canonical provider
    response.
  source_labels:
  - '{run2}'
  - '{prov}'
- concept_id: C-017
  name: Public Command Contract
  definition: The minimum stable command surface exposed by LMRS for health, 
    capacity, admission, chat, queue, cache, and model lifecycle operations.
  properties:
  - The minimal runtime command set includes healthcheck, model status, 
    capacity, token counting, dry-run estimate/admission, chat request, queue 
    status, and request cancellation.
  - The estimate command performs dry-run admission and returns would_execute, 
    would_queue, or would_reject with reason code, token breakdown, and capacity
    snapshot.
  - The chat command performs token counting and admission, then returns 
    executed, queued, or rejected with the appropriate result, queue state, or 
    stable reason code.
  - Additional cache and memory commands include local_model_cache_preload, 
    local_model_cache_status, local_model_cache_delete, local_model_load, 
    local_model_unload, and local_model_reload.
  source_labels:
  - '{cmd1}'
  - '{cmd2}'
  - '{cmd3}'
  - '{cmd4}'
- concept_id: C-018
  name: Structured Error Contract
  definition: The stable machine-readable error code set returned by LMRS for 
    admission, tokenizer, hardware, model lifecycle, runtime, cache, and 
    cancellation failures.
  properties:
  - Minimum errors include CONTEXT_OVERFLOW, REQUEST_TOO_LARGE, 
    HARDWARE_VRAM_INSUFFICIENT, HARDWARE_CAPACITY_UNKNOWN, 
    TOKENIZER_UNAVAILABLE, MODEL_BUSY_AND_QUEUE_FULL, MODEL_NOT_LOADED, 
    MODEL_LOAD_FAILED, RUNTIME_CALL_FAILED, and REQUEST_CANCELLED.
  - Additional errors include MODEL_NOT_CACHED, MODEL_CACHE_PRELOAD_FAILED, 
    MODEL_CACHE_CORRUPTED, MODEL_ALREADY_LOADED, MODEL_UNLOAD_FAILED, 
    LMCACHE_UNAVAILABLE, LMCACHE_LOOKUP_FAILED, and LMCACHE_WRITE_FAILED.
  source_labels:
  - '{err1}'
  - '{err2}'
- concept_id: C-019
  name: Telemetry
  definition: The observable data emitted and returned by LMRS for debugging, 
    admission traceability, and calibration feedback.
  properties:
  - Telemetry includes predicted tokens, predicted VRAM, actual prompt tokens, 
    actual output tokens, latency, queue wait, VRAM snapshots, admission 
    verdict, and reason code.
  - Telemetry supports calibration of KV-cache cost, per-request overhead, 
    safety margin, and scheduler policy.
  - LMCache telemetry includes hit/miss, reused tokens, new tokens, lookup 
    latency, write latency, eviction events, and impact on actual VRAM and 
    latency.
  source_labels:
  - '{obs1}'
  - '{obs2}'
  - '{lmcache7}'
- concept_id: C-020
  name: Calibration Profile
  definition: The model, runtime, quantization, and hardware-specific 
    calibration data for estimating KV-cache and overhead costs.
  properties:
  - KV-cache cost per token supports empirical calibration.
  - The preferred calibration method measures VRAM growth across prompt lengths.
  - Calibration profiles are bound to model, runtime backend, 
    quantization/profile, and hardware profile.
  - Changing model, runtime, or GPU invalidates or requires renewing the 
    calibration profile.
  source_labels:
  - '{cal1}'
  - '{cal2}'
- concept_id: C-021
  name: Runtime Configuration
  definition: The service configuration that defines hardware, resident 
    services, safety reserves, model profiles, runtime backend, tokenizer, queue
    policy, and queue limits.
  properties:
  - Configuration includes hardware profile, resident services, safety margin, 
    runtime reserve, models, runtime backend, tokenizer profile, queue policy, 
    and queue limits.
  - For vLLM, configuration includes LMCache enablement, cache storage path, 
    policy, limits, eviction policy, namespace/session binding, and 
    compatibility mode with model profile.
  source_labels:
  - '{cfg1}'
  - '{lmcache3}'
- concept_id: C-022
  name: MVP Scope
  definition: The explicit boundary of features included and excluded from the 
    initial LMRS implementation.
  properties:
  - MVP includes one resident LLM on one GPU, persistent vectorizer/chunker 
    services, service baseline measurement, model static measurement, 
    tokenizer-aware counting, admission, largest-fit queue, one selected runtime
    backend, and adapter-based MCP commands.
  - MVP excludes multi-GPU scheduling, multiple resident LLMs, streaming, 
    speculative decoding, distributed inference, automatic model eviction, and 
    advanced batching optimizer.
  - MVP includes disk cache lifecycle, explicit memory load/unload, VRAM 
    measurement after load, and basic vLLM LMCache configuration support.
  source_labels:
  - '{mvp1}'
  - '{mvp2}'
  - '{mvp3}'
- concept_id: C-023
  name: Disk Model Cache
  definition: The disk-resident model cache that stores model weights, tokenizer
    files, runtime metadata, integrity information, readiness state, and cache 
    profile independently from VRAM residency.
  properties:
  - Preload downloads, verifies integrity, prepares local layout, tokenizer 
    files, runtime metadata, and readiness marker without requiring VRAM load.
  - Disk cache and VRAM model residency are separate states.
  - Model states include not_cached, caching, cached_on_disk, loading_to_memory,
    loaded_in_memory, unloading, and failed.
  - Cache management commands add, check, delete, list, verify integrity, and 
    update metadata.
  - Cache profile stores model_name, runtime, model_path, quantization/profile, 
    declared_context_window, tokenizer profile, checksum or revision, disk size,
    load date, compatibility flags, and cache status.
  source_labels:
  - '{dcache1}'
  - '{dcache2}'
  - '{dcache3}'
  - '{dcache4}'
  - '{inv4}'
- concept_id: C-024
  name: Model Memory Lifecycle
  definition: The lifecycle that explicitly loads, unloads, reloads, and reports
    model residency in memory independently from disk cache availability.
  properties:
  - Loading to memory is separate from disk cache preload.
  - Lifecycle commands include load_model, unload_model, reload_model, 
    model_status, and model_lifecycle_status.
  - Before loading, the service verifies disk cache presence and runtime 
    compatibility or returns MODEL_NOT_CACHED unless preload is explicitly 
    allowed.
  - After loading, the service measures model_loaded_free_vram and records 
    measured_model_static_vram.
  - Unloading frees VRAM residency without requiring disk cache deletion and 
    updates free VRAM state.
  - The MVP policy is keep_loaded for one resident LLM until explicit unload or 
    replacement.
  source_labels:
  - '{mlife1}'
  - '{mlife2}'
  - '{mlife3}'
  - '{mlife4}'
  - '{mlife5}'
  - '{mlife6}'
  - '{inv4}'
- concept_id: C-025
  name: LMCache Integration
  definition: The vLLM LMCache runtime caching integration used to accelerate 
    reuse of repeated or long context while preserving conservative admission 
    decisions.
  properties:
  - LMCache is a separate runtime/caching backend and does not replace admission
    control.
  - Disk model cache and LMCache are distinct caches with different artifacts.
  - Estimate responses may include cache_reuse_possible, 
    estimated_cache_hit_tokens, effective_new_tokens, and cache_estimate_quality
    when pre-execution cache information is available.
  - If a cache hit is not guaranteed before launch, admission estimates the 
    request as a full cache miss.
  source_labels:
  - '{lmcache1}'
  - '{lmcache2}'
  - '{lmcache3}'
  - '{lmcache4}'
  - '{lmcache5}'
  - '{lmcache6}'
  - '{lmcache7}'
  - '{inv5}'
